1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
<font color="#20e020">Cleaned and corrected by Lunar Team</font>

2
00:00:25,609 --> 00:00:27,099
Oh, Frank.

3
00:00:27,311 --> 00:00:29,609
You're so bad.

4
00:00:34,351 --> 00:00:35,375
You sure about this?

5
00:00:35,552 --> 00:00:38,077
- What if somebody sees us?
- Relax, baby.

6
00:00:38,255 --> 00:00:39,620
I got it all under control.

7
00:01:06,383 --> 00:01:07,611
Yeah.

8
00:01:35,178 --> 00:01:36,236
What do we have here?

9
00:01:36,413 --> 00:01:38,574
Harry Lashley,
a junior VP with the company.

10
00:01:38,749 --> 00:01:40,910
He's the son-in-law of the boss,
A.P. Caid.

11
00:01:41,084 --> 00:01:44,417
The receptionist, Miss Duane,
forgot her phone, came back to get it...

12
00:01:44,588 --> 00:01:48,149
- ...found him like this.
- She called Security. I was working late.

13
00:01:48,325 --> 00:01:50,088
Called 911 as soon as I got here.

14
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:53,491
I then opened up Mr. Caid's office
to check that nothing was amiss.

15
00:01:53,663 --> 00:01:57,326
I have an all-access pass.
Well, come on.

16
00:01:59,202 --> 00:02:02,694
Only one thing was missing, but
it's the most valuable painting he owns.

17
00:02:04,107 --> 00:02:06,769
A.P. picked it up at an auction
a couple months ago.

18
00:02:06,943 --> 00:02:08,376
Paid a little over 50 million.

19
00:02:08,545 --> 00:02:11,673
But you said that these doors
were closed before you got here?

20
00:02:11,848 --> 00:02:14,009
The office is locked up
when A.P.'s not here.

21
00:02:14,184 --> 00:02:16,880
It's a strict rule that A.P. has
when he's gone.

22
00:02:17,053 --> 00:02:19,544
And Mr. Lashley,
what would he have been doing here?

23
00:02:19,723 --> 00:02:21,987
No idea.
His office is two floors down.

24
00:02:22,159 --> 00:02:24,650
- He had no reason to be here.
- Maybe he was involved.

25
00:02:24,828 --> 00:02:28,229
If we're lucky, we'll find the weapon.
This was obviously well-planned.

26
00:02:28,432 --> 00:02:30,730
- Why is that obvious?
- Nobody finds themselves...

27
00:02:30,934 --> 00:02:34,995
...in a locked room full of old masters
in the middle of the night by accident.

28
00:02:35,172 --> 00:02:36,196
So, well-planned.

29
00:02:36,373 --> 00:02:39,365
But they weren't expecting
to meet any opposition...

30
00:02:39,543 --> 00:02:42,569
...otherwise Mr. Lashley here
would've been shot or tasered...

31
00:02:42,746 --> 00:02:45,044
...or tied up and gagged.

32
00:02:45,215 --> 00:02:49,151
But when they were surprised by him,
they simply hit him on the head.

33
00:02:49,319 --> 00:02:51,514
Which suggests
an improvised weapon, yeah?

34
00:02:53,190 --> 00:02:57,217
- I'm listening.
- Yes. Something like...

35
00:03:03,967 --> 00:03:09,098
I predict Forensics will find
traces of Harry Lashley on this bust.

36
00:03:09,272 --> 00:03:11,706
No offense,
but I used to be police myself...

37
00:03:11,875 --> 00:03:15,072
...and that's not police work,
that's guessing.

38
00:03:17,013 --> 00:03:20,346
- Carl, bag this for me.
- Yes, sir.

39
00:03:22,552 --> 00:03:25,419
Frank, is there a good diner
around here?

40
00:03:25,589 --> 00:03:30,390
Uh. Yeah. Garrity's.
A couple blocks over on Madison.

41
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:32,187
- Do they do good eggs?
- I guess.

42
00:03:32,362 --> 00:03:34,421
That's the test of a good diner. Eggs.

43
00:03:35,765 --> 00:03:38,563
You must've had a contentious
relationship with your father.

44
00:03:38,735 --> 00:03:39,861
Excuse me? No.

45
00:03:40,036 --> 00:03:43,699
Tell me, I can't tell with
the hair in her face, and the sobbing...

46
00:03:43,874 --> 00:03:47,401
...but I imagine Miss Duane's
quite attractive, isn't she?

47
00:03:48,078 --> 00:03:50,774
I mean, receptionists are often hired
for their looks.

48
00:03:50,947 --> 00:03:52,278
What are you talking about?

49
00:03:52,449 --> 00:03:55,009
- Where are you going with this?
- This way.

50
00:03:55,752 --> 00:03:57,583
Look at the seams.

51
00:03:58,121 --> 00:04:01,284
These shoulder seams here
are pulled apart.

52
00:04:01,458 --> 00:04:04,120
And the way
he's lying so straight like this.

53
00:04:04,294 --> 00:04:07,127
- And that shoe that has come off.
- Yeah, we all noticed.

54
00:04:07,297 --> 00:04:11,063
- Somebody must've dragged him here.
- But dragged him from where? And why?

55
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:14,931
Care to tell us, Frank?

56
00:04:15,639 --> 00:04:17,266
I don't know
what you're talking about.

57
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:19,965
Oh. All right, then let me explain.

58
00:04:20,143 --> 00:04:23,169
You and Miss Duane came here
to have sex in the boss's office.

59
00:04:23,346 --> 00:04:25,337
Why would I do such a stupid thing?

60
00:04:25,549 --> 00:04:28,017
Because it's forbidden
and thus very sexy, of course.

61
00:04:28,184 --> 00:04:31,585
Especially if you have father issues.
And who doesn't?

62
00:04:31,755 --> 00:04:35,782
It's a furtive but powerful challenge
to the paternal authority.

63
00:04:35,959 --> 00:04:38,359
So you open up A.P.'s office,
and then:

64
00:04:38,562 --> 00:04:41,122
"Crap, there's a dead body.
What are we gonna do?"

65
00:04:41,298 --> 00:04:44,825
You gotta report it, but how do you
explain your presence in his office?

66
00:04:45,001 --> 00:04:47,492
You drag the body out...

67
00:04:47,671 --> 00:04:50,697
...and lock the doors back up
before calling the police.

68
00:04:50,874 --> 00:04:52,865
- Who is this guy?
- He's a pain in the ass.

69
00:04:53,076 --> 00:04:56,204
He's making sense. You sure you don't
wanna change your statement?

70
00:04:56,379 --> 00:04:58,040
- No.
- Oh, please, Frank.

71
00:04:58,214 --> 00:04:59,681
It'd be silly to deny it.

72
00:04:59,849 --> 00:05:02,682
Five minutes in the room
with the persuasive Agent Cho...

73
00:05:02,852 --> 00:05:05,685
...and Miss Duane will tell all.

74
00:05:05,855 --> 00:05:08,983
- Back off, man.
- I want to, believe me.

75
00:05:09,159 --> 00:05:12,151
I have zero interest in your sex life.
One thing occurs to me:

76
00:05:12,329 --> 00:05:14,797
A room like this
would have security cameras in it.

77
00:05:14,965 --> 00:05:16,830
- Yeah?
- Yes.

78
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,696
But surely you wouldn't
knowingly record yourself...

79
00:05:19,869 --> 00:05:22,804
...making love on the boss's carpet.

80
00:05:22,973 --> 00:05:25,339
You'd have to know
that the cameras were off.

81
00:05:25,508 --> 00:05:27,806
You turned them off,
being the head of Security.

82
00:05:27,978 --> 00:05:30,606
They are off, aren't they, Frank?

83
00:05:31,147 --> 00:05:34,708
So the question now is, Frank,
how long have they been off...

84
00:05:34,884 --> 00:05:37,751
...and who else might've known
they were gonna be off?

85
00:05:37,921 --> 00:05:40,082
You guys can take it from here.
I am starving.

86
00:05:40,256 --> 00:05:42,486
I gotta get me some eggs.
Garrity's, right?

87
00:05:42,659 --> 00:05:45,628
- Thanks.
- You ready to tell us the truth?

88
00:05:57,874 --> 00:05:59,398
Hey, look at this.

89
00:05:59,609 --> 00:06:02,840
It's known as <i>The Moro,</i>
from 14th century Italy.

90
00:06:03,013 --> 00:06:05,379
It's a portrait of somebody
named Carlotta Moro.

91
00:06:05,548 --> 00:06:08,642
It's the only known works of an artist
known as the Sienese Master.

92
00:06:08,818 --> 00:06:11,252
It's been owned by two popes,
the king of France...

93
00:06:11,421 --> 00:06:13,321
...and John Jacob Astor,
among others.

94
00:06:13,490 --> 00:06:16,118
Then A.P. Caid bought it for $50 million
three months ago.

95
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:18,540
What do we know about Caid?

96
00:06:18,662 --> 00:06:20,892
Apart from
he's an idiot with his money?

97
00:06:21,765 --> 00:06:23,494
Like the cover says:

98
00:06:23,667 --> 00:06:25,999
Got his start
as a roughneck in the oil fields...

99
00:06:26,169 --> 00:06:28,899
...then turned into a wildcatter
and made a fortune.

100
00:06:29,072 --> 00:06:32,564
His trick's pulling oil out of places
nobody else has the balls to go.

101
00:06:32,742 --> 00:06:34,937
- Where is he now?
- Somewhere on a sailboat.

102
00:06:35,145 --> 00:06:37,443
He's been told what's going on.
He's coming back.

103
00:06:37,647 --> 00:06:40,445
It would've been easy for him
to sail up the Coast...

104
00:06:40,650 --> 00:06:43,744
...put into a small marina,
then come back and commit the crime.

105
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:44,944
Why would he do that?

106
00:06:45,155 --> 00:06:47,783
I'm saying he could.
Maybe he wants the insurance money.

107
00:06:47,957 --> 00:06:49,857
Or he wants to get rid of Harry.

108
00:06:50,026 --> 00:06:51,891
Did you check
with Caid's security team?

109
00:06:52,062 --> 00:06:55,930
Everybody that didn't wear a suit knew
about Frank and his use of A.P.'s office.

110
00:06:56,099 --> 00:06:58,431
Any number of people might've known
the cameras were off.

111
00:06:58,601 --> 00:07:01,866
Sounds like we're gonna have
to check out any number of people.

112
00:07:02,672 --> 00:07:04,264
- Where's Jane?
- I don't know.

113
00:07:05,075 --> 00:07:08,306
Well, find him.
I'm gonna go and talk to Lashley's wife.

114
00:07:08,478 --> 00:07:11,072
You guys give Caid's personnel files
a good hard look.

115
00:07:11,247 --> 00:07:13,215
See if there's
any criminal connections.

116
00:07:13,383 --> 00:07:15,715
- Especially Harry Lashley.
- You think he's dirty?

117
00:07:15,885 --> 00:07:16,909
Well, he's involved.

118
00:07:17,087 --> 00:07:19,885
Why else would he be there
in the middle of the night?

119
00:07:23,126 --> 00:07:24,593
It doesn't feel real.

120
00:07:24,761 --> 00:07:27,992
If it wasn't for you people,
I wouldn't believe it.

121
00:07:28,198 --> 00:07:32,157
Harry's going to walk through the door,
and this is all just a strange dream.

122
00:07:32,335 --> 00:07:34,929
But here you are. Harry's dead.

123
00:07:35,105 --> 00:07:36,970
When was the last time
you spoke to him?

124
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:41,406
Yesterday afternoon. Just chitchat.
Where to go for dinner.

125
00:07:41,578 --> 00:07:44,570
Then he left me a message canceling.
Didn't say why.

126
00:07:44,748 --> 00:07:47,808
That wasn't unusual?
Canceling without giving any reason?

127
00:07:47,984 --> 00:07:51,579
No. Harry was very hard-working.
Very keen.

128
00:07:51,755 --> 00:07:53,985
Stuff would come up all the time.

129
00:07:54,624 --> 00:07:56,455
What was his job here, exactly?

130
00:07:57,127 --> 00:07:58,492
Good question.

131
00:07:58,661 --> 00:08:02,529
He was a sort of jack-of-all-trades
for my father.

132
00:08:02,732 --> 00:08:04,962
Did whatever he needed
from week to week.

133
00:08:05,135 --> 00:08:07,126
My father liked
having someone on hand...

134
00:08:07,303 --> 00:08:10,033
...with no ties or friendships
within the company.

135
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:11,639
That's a tough assignment.

136
00:08:12,142 --> 00:08:13,507
Yes.

137
00:08:13,676 --> 00:08:16,702
But he only joined the company
when we were married.

138
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:20,646
- He was learning the ropes.
- Your father didn't want Harry...

139
00:08:20,817 --> 00:08:23,843
...to have a soft ride
just because he was your husband.

140
00:08:24,020 --> 00:08:25,681
You could put it like that.

141
00:08:25,855 --> 00:08:29,518
- What was his previous employment?
- He was a musician.

142
00:08:29,692 --> 00:08:31,990
- Jazz guitarist.
- Yes.

143
00:08:32,162 --> 00:08:35,461
- How did you know that?
- I looked at his fingers. Guitarist fingers.

144
00:08:35,632 --> 00:08:39,864
Jazz with a flamenco edge,
judging by the ridge on his thumb.

145
00:08:40,036 --> 00:08:41,060
Yes.

146
00:08:41,271 --> 00:08:45,469
He was playing a gig at one of these
ghastly events you have to go to...

147
00:08:45,642 --> 00:08:47,507
...and you struck up a conversation.

148
00:08:47,677 --> 00:08:50,407
You love jazz,
so you could talk with him easily...

149
00:08:50,580 --> 00:08:55,040
...which normally is quite difficult
for you, outside of business.

150
00:08:55,218 --> 00:08:58,517
I... Well, yes, that's... But...

151
00:08:58,688 --> 00:09:01,350
I'm sorry, Miss Caid.
He's just showing off.

152
00:09:02,225 --> 00:09:05,854
Can you think of any reason
why Harry would've been on this floor...

153
00:09:06,029 --> 00:09:08,054
...at that time of night?

154
00:09:08,565 --> 00:09:10,396
No.

155
00:09:10,567 --> 00:09:13,661
He would've known
that neither I nor my father were here.

156
00:09:13,837 --> 00:09:15,099
So?

157
00:09:15,305 --> 00:09:18,240
I understand, from my assistant,
that Frank's misbehavior...

158
00:09:18,408 --> 00:09:21,502
...was an open secret
amongst the junior staff.

159
00:09:21,678 --> 00:09:23,669
He's no longer a Caid employee.

160
00:09:24,247 --> 00:09:29,583
Perhaps Harry had found out,
and he was trying to catch him.

161
00:09:29,752 --> 00:09:30,844
Perhaps.

162
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:33,352
Or is it possible
that he had prior knowledge...

163
00:09:33,523 --> 00:09:35,354
...that the theft
was gonna take place?

164
00:09:35,525 --> 00:09:38,221
You're asking if he
could've been complicit in the theft?

165
00:09:38,394 --> 00:09:40,453
- Yes, I am.
- No.

166
00:09:40,630 --> 00:09:44,623
He was honest and gentle. No.

167
00:09:44,834 --> 00:09:46,802
He never talked to you
about the painting?

168
00:09:46,970 --> 00:09:50,133
No. I didn't approve of buying it.

169
00:09:50,340 --> 00:09:52,467
Fifty million dollars
is far too much money...

170
00:09:52,642 --> 00:09:54,735
...to spend on a single object.

171
00:09:54,911 --> 00:09:59,610
When it comes to art,
rational arguments don't apply for him.

172
00:09:59,782 --> 00:10:02,114
That would be Kathryn Hawkes'
area of expertise.

173
00:10:02,285 --> 00:10:04,776
Kathryn Hawkes? She is?

174
00:10:04,954 --> 00:10:08,481
She buys the art
for my father's collection.

175
00:10:09,592 --> 00:10:11,822
It's a terrible tragedy. Terrible.

176
00:10:11,995 --> 00:10:13,963
- Did you know Mr. Lashley?
- Not well.

177
00:10:14,130 --> 00:10:16,997
I mean, I knew of him,
obviously, but...

178
00:10:17,166 --> 00:10:20,465
- Poor man. It's very sad.
- But you think the loss of the painting...

179
00:10:20,637 --> 00:10:23,435
- ...is the real tragedy here.
- Well, no.

180
00:10:23,606 --> 00:10:26,336
Every human life is a sacred thing.

181
00:10:26,509 --> 00:10:29,307
But <i>The Moro</i>
is a uniquely beautiful treasure.

182
00:10:29,479 --> 00:10:31,970
- Beyond precious.
- You know the art market.

183
00:10:32,148 --> 00:10:35,117
Who'd be willing and able
to steal such a high-end piece?

184
00:10:35,285 --> 00:10:37,947
Worldwide, there's probably
a good hundred collectors...

185
00:10:38,121 --> 00:10:41,989
...with the resources and passion enough
to do something like this.

186
00:10:42,158 --> 00:10:46,618
But it's public knowledge that Mr. Caid
outbid Shirali Arlov to get <i>The Moro</i>.

187
00:10:46,796 --> 00:10:50,994
- And Mr. Arlov was not pleased.
- Shirali Arlov, the Russian oil baron?

188
00:10:51,167 --> 00:10:52,634
Him.

189
00:10:54,671 --> 00:10:56,605
Arlov is a nasty piece of work.

190
00:10:56,773 --> 00:11:00,470
He has a legit front as an oilman,
but he's a super-rich gangster, basically.

191
00:11:00,643 --> 00:11:03,635
He likes his business rivals
to be delivered to him hogtied...

192
00:11:03,813 --> 00:11:05,804
...so he can kill them personally.

193
00:11:05,982 --> 00:11:08,780
Vicious, greedy,
and the owner of the finest collection...

194
00:11:08,952 --> 00:11:10,783
...of Renaissance paintings in Russia.

195
00:11:10,954 --> 00:11:13,445
- Our kind of guy.
- Talk to the Organized Crime boys.

196
00:11:13,623 --> 00:11:17,320
Find out who Arlov's connections are,
who he would use for a job like this...

197
00:11:17,493 --> 00:11:20,053
- ...and where they were last night.
- You got it, boss.

198
00:11:20,830 --> 00:11:23,458
- What do you have?
- Harry Lashley has a past.

199
00:11:23,633 --> 00:11:28,002
Ten years ago, in Canada, he stole cash
from a video store he was working at.

200
00:11:28,171 --> 00:11:31,402
- Busted, and served six months.
- Damn. I was hoping he was clean.

201
00:11:31,574 --> 00:11:32,632
Yeah, I know.

202
00:11:32,809 --> 00:11:36,870
Then I tried to find any connection
he had to the art world. This popped up.

203
00:11:37,046 --> 00:11:40,345
A month ago, he paid a company
called R.W. Arts $20,000.

204
00:11:40,516 --> 00:11:43,246
That's the only financial transaction
he's made recently.

205
00:11:43,453 --> 00:11:46,911
- What's R.W. Arts?
- I don't know yet. A shell, probably.

206
00:11:47,090 --> 00:11:49,422
The address is just a mailbox
in Lockesdale.

207
00:11:49,592 --> 00:11:52,060
- Little farming town up north.
- That's interesting.

208
00:11:52,228 --> 00:11:54,822
Let's, Van Pelt and I,
go up and take a look-see.

209
00:11:55,865 --> 00:11:57,059
You and Van Pelt?

210
00:11:57,967 --> 00:12:00,868
Well, she's from a small farming town.
Aren't you?

211
00:12:01,037 --> 00:12:02,095
Yes, I am.

212
00:12:02,271 --> 00:12:05,707
Right. Well, that's what you guys call...
What is it? Cover.

213
00:12:07,043 --> 00:12:10,501
What do you need cover for?
You're a CBI operative.

214
00:12:10,680 --> 00:12:12,978
I thought I was a "consultant".

215
00:12:14,217 --> 00:12:16,185
Just follow procedure. Have fun.

216
00:12:17,420 --> 00:12:18,717
Where's the fun in that?

217
00:12:19,922 --> 00:12:21,287
Shall we?

218
00:12:47,450 --> 00:12:49,247
How cute is this?

219
00:12:50,386 --> 00:12:53,048
Sure doesn't look like
the lair of high-end art thieves.

220
00:12:53,222 --> 00:12:56,157
Not at all. Flowers and fruit.

221
00:12:58,795 --> 00:13:00,956
"Rob Wallace Gallery and Framing."

222
00:13:01,764 --> 00:13:03,527
Bingo.

223
00:13:04,967 --> 00:13:06,457
Let's not start with the badge.

224
00:13:06,636 --> 00:13:10,766
Let's get the lay of land first,
then we use the badge if we have to.

225
00:13:10,940 --> 00:13:12,271
Okay.

226
00:13:12,442 --> 00:13:15,172
- But don't embarrass me.
- What do you mean?

227
00:13:15,344 --> 00:13:16,743
You know what I mean.

228
00:13:16,913 --> 00:13:18,141
No. What do you mean?

229
00:13:21,150 --> 00:13:23,584
- Morning, folks.
- Hi.

230
00:13:23,753 --> 00:13:25,482
Look around. Make yourself at home.

231
00:13:26,089 --> 00:13:27,681
Will do.

232
00:13:43,773 --> 00:13:45,570
- Hi.
- Hi.

233
00:13:47,677 --> 00:13:49,144
I know what you're thinking.

234
00:13:50,213 --> 00:13:51,373
You do?

235
00:13:51,581 --> 00:13:54,379
You're thinking
my fiancée's a lot younger than me.

236
00:13:54,951 --> 00:13:59,012
- Oh, no.
- Well, she is.

237
00:13:59,188 --> 00:14:02,282
I'll let you in on a little secret.
I was her college professor.

238
00:14:02,725 --> 00:14:03,783
Seduced her.

239
00:14:06,529 --> 00:14:09,692
- No kidding?
- Mm-hm. Parents hated it. Lot of drama.

240
00:14:09,866 --> 00:14:13,029
- Totally worth it, though, right, babe?
- Okay, that's what I mean.

241
00:14:13,202 --> 00:14:14,726
Come on, stop it.

242
00:14:14,904 --> 00:14:16,633
Oh, that's good. He's good.

243
00:14:20,610 --> 00:14:24,068
Wow. Think you could do a portrait
of my fiancée?

244
00:14:24,247 --> 00:14:25,407
- No.
- Yeah, you bet.

245
00:14:25,615 --> 00:14:27,640
- No.
- Who's the boss in this relationship?

246
00:14:28,651 --> 00:14:30,551
He's just playing the fool.

247
00:14:31,254 --> 00:14:32,983
Stop it now.

248
00:14:40,129 --> 00:14:42,563
Okay, turn just a little to your left.

249
00:14:42,732 --> 00:14:45,132
There. Good.

250
00:14:48,204 --> 00:14:49,933
Looks good.

251
00:14:51,674 --> 00:14:54,575
Just make sure you don't do her eyes
too close together.

252
00:14:56,579 --> 00:14:58,979
Um... Um...

253
00:14:59,515 --> 00:15:03,781
I'm sorry. I'm getting in your space.
Where's your facilities?

254
00:15:03,953 --> 00:15:08,185
- Down the hall, door on the right.
- Okay. All right. Okay.

255
00:15:08,357 --> 00:15:11,019
Just not too close, you know?
You know what I'm saying.

256
00:15:15,298 --> 00:15:16,390
Yeah.

257
00:15:24,607 --> 00:15:29,943
- Just passing through town, are you?
- Yes. Just passing through.

258
00:15:45,061 --> 00:15:47,495
- You have an excellent nose.
- Thank you.

259
00:16:02,578 --> 00:16:05,809
- How long have you lived here?
- All my life.

260
00:16:21,831 --> 00:16:23,992
Do you mind if I ask you
a personal question?

261
00:16:24,166 --> 00:16:27,226
That depends, I suppose. Go ahead.

262
00:16:28,371 --> 00:16:30,091
How long have you and Patrick
been together?

263
00:16:30,139 --> 00:16:33,267
Um... Not long.

264
00:16:33,442 --> 00:16:36,309
I'm just gonna say it.
Are you sure about marrying this guy?

265
00:16:36,479 --> 00:16:38,879
I mean, he kind of appears
to be a jerk.

266
00:16:39,048 --> 00:16:42,381
- I mean, no offense.
- Oh, no, yes.

267
00:16:42,551 --> 00:16:45,884
I'm not marrying him. That's... No.

268
00:16:46,055 --> 00:16:48,023
Don't worry about that.
Not gonna happen.

269
00:17:10,346 --> 00:17:11,370
Hello.

270
00:17:11,647 --> 00:17:14,741
Turn your head just to the left
a little bit.

271
00:17:14,917 --> 00:17:18,353
Just a little bit more.
A little bit more to your left.

272
00:17:18,888 --> 00:17:22,153
Just a... That's great. Hold that, okay?

273
00:17:22,325 --> 00:17:25,385
Look down. Hold that.

274
00:17:25,561 --> 00:17:29,554
This way I'm gonna have
a lot of light on your cheekbones.

275
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:34,660
You're gonna be so amazed
with how good this looks.

276
00:17:35,338 --> 00:17:37,431
Your facial structure.

277
00:17:40,076 --> 00:17:42,943
Okay, stand up.

278
00:17:56,525 --> 00:17:58,755
Put your hands in the air.

279
00:17:59,161 --> 00:18:00,719
Okay.

280
00:18:03,165 --> 00:18:04,564
Move. Move!

281
00:18:04,834 --> 00:18:07,394
Steady. Steady.

282
00:18:09,171 --> 00:18:12,163
Mr. Wallace, if you would just let me
show you my badge.

283
00:18:12,341 --> 00:18:14,832
We're from the California Bureau
of Investigation.

284
00:18:15,010 --> 00:18:18,571
Our office knows we're here.
If you kill us, they'll know who did it.

285
00:18:18,748 --> 00:18:20,739
Kill you? Why would I wanna kill you?

286
00:18:20,916 --> 00:18:22,907
You're cops?

287
00:18:24,887 --> 00:18:27,219
We're investigating
the murder of Harry Lashley.

288
00:18:27,390 --> 00:18:28,880
Lashley's dead?

289
00:18:32,528 --> 00:18:33,893
What happened? I...

290
00:18:34,063 --> 00:18:37,294
You might tell us that,
considering you have the stolen painting.

291
00:18:37,500 --> 00:18:39,365
No. That's... That's a copy.

292
00:18:39,535 --> 00:18:42,868
Mr. Wallace here is an art forger.

293
00:18:43,305 --> 00:18:45,739
A very, very good one, by the way.

294
00:18:45,908 --> 00:18:49,207
That's a copy. He's not a thief.

295
00:18:49,879 --> 00:18:52,780
- Well, then, give me the shotgun.
- Oh.

296
00:18:59,054 --> 00:19:00,954
Mr. Lashley came to me months ago...

297
00:19:01,123 --> 00:19:03,148
...and commissioned two copies
of <i>The Moro.</i>

298
00:19:04,093 --> 00:19:05,560
How did he find you?

299
00:19:06,061 --> 00:19:09,622
People who need my services
tend to find me eventually.

300
00:19:11,333 --> 00:19:13,130
It's not a crime, what I do.

301
00:19:13,302 --> 00:19:17,295
If you have a valuable painting, it's
sensible to have a copy made for display.

302
00:19:17,473 --> 00:19:19,566
So that way the real thing's safe.

303
00:19:20,042 --> 00:19:22,306
He picked up the copies
about a month ago.

304
00:19:22,478 --> 00:19:25,413
So you gave him two copies already?

305
00:19:25,581 --> 00:19:29,142
- What's this one for?
- This one, I did for myself.

306
00:19:30,319 --> 00:19:32,617
It's a beautiful picture.
I'd come to love her.

307
00:19:33,956 --> 00:19:36,481
- Why did he want two copies?
- He didn't say.

308
00:19:36,659 --> 00:19:38,559
And you didn't ask questions.

309
00:19:39,762 --> 00:19:41,525
He paid me $10,000
for each of them...

310
00:19:41,697 --> 00:19:44,461
...so, no, I didn't ask any questions.

311
00:19:44,633 --> 00:19:47,431
How do we know
this isn't the real thing?

312
00:19:47,870 --> 00:19:49,861
I'm not a forger, I'm an artist.

313
00:19:50,072 --> 00:19:52,267
I'm not trying to trick anyone, okay?

314
00:19:52,441 --> 00:19:54,033
I add a detail that ensures...

315
00:19:54,210 --> 00:19:56,838
...that nobody can sell my work
as the genuine article.

316
00:19:57,079 --> 00:19:59,206
That's very ethical of you.

317
00:19:59,982 --> 00:20:01,950
It looks exactly the same to me.

318
00:20:02,117 --> 00:20:04,347
No, he's telling the truth.
Take a closer look.

319
00:20:08,724 --> 00:20:11,124
- What am I looking for?
- Closer.

320
00:20:13,562 --> 00:20:17,328
- Ooh. I still don't...
- You're getting warmer.

321
00:20:19,668 --> 00:20:23,104
Oh, a TV aerial.

322
00:20:23,906 --> 00:20:25,305
- Yes.
- They didn't have TV.

323
00:20:25,474 --> 00:20:28,136
No. Beautiful work.

324
00:20:28,677 --> 00:20:31,840
- Can I borrow it for a while?
- I don't know about that.

325
00:20:32,014 --> 00:20:34,312
How do I know I'm gonna get it back?

326
00:20:34,483 --> 00:20:36,508
Let me put it another way.

327
00:20:36,685 --> 00:20:40,018
Lend it to me or Agent Van Pelt here
will take you and the painting...

328
00:20:40,189 --> 00:20:41,986
...back to Sacramento...

329
00:20:42,157 --> 00:20:45,991
...and ask you mind-numbing questions
for hours and hours on end.

330
00:20:48,497 --> 00:20:49,521
Thank you.

331
00:20:50,933 --> 00:20:52,525
Copies, huh? Clever.

332
00:20:52,701 --> 00:20:55,192
Harry and his accomplices
steal the painting once...

333
00:20:55,371 --> 00:20:58,033
- ...but they get to sell it three times.
- Hey, boss.

334
00:20:58,274 --> 00:21:01,004
A.P. Caid's back from his trip.
On his way to his office.

335
00:21:07,683 --> 00:21:09,617
No, no, no. You got it all wrong.

336
00:21:09,785 --> 00:21:12,549
See, I told Harry to get a copy made
on the hush-hush.

337
00:21:12,721 --> 00:21:16,122
I mean, what kind of idiot is gonna
put $50,000,000 on his wall...

338
00:21:16,292 --> 00:21:19,921
- ...so anybody can steal it?
- The painting that was stolen is a fake?

339
00:21:20,596 --> 00:21:22,223
Damn right it was.

340
00:21:22,398 --> 00:21:24,798
Now, the real one
is in my private vault.

341
00:21:24,967 --> 00:21:27,959
With all due respect, A.P.,
the art here is my responsibility.

342
00:21:28,170 --> 00:21:30,400
Kathryn, the fewer people who knew,
the better.

343
00:21:30,573 --> 00:21:32,370
Besides, it paid off, didn't it?

344
00:21:32,741 --> 00:21:35,608
Right now, there's some
thieving son of a bitch out there...

345
00:21:35,778 --> 00:21:37,803
...who spent a whole lot of money
on junk.

346
00:21:37,980 --> 00:21:42,747
- Daddy, Harry's dead.
- Oh, baby, I know. I'm sorry.

347
00:21:42,918 --> 00:21:46,649
I know, it's too sad,
but, you know, I did like that kid.

348
00:21:46,822 --> 00:21:49,814
- Can we see the painting?
- You wanna see my baby?

349
00:21:49,992 --> 00:21:51,254
I'll open the vault.

350
00:21:56,565 --> 00:21:57,589
Ain't she a beauty?

351
00:21:58,067 --> 00:22:00,831
- You mind if I take a look here?
- No, no, yeah, yeah.

352
00:22:02,237 --> 00:22:04,068
A masterpiece
of quattrocento portraiture.

353
00:22:04,239 --> 00:22:07,003
- One of a kind.
- Fifty million dollars right there.

354
00:22:07,409 --> 00:22:10,776
That's about $10,000 right there.
It's a fake.

355
00:22:11,146 --> 00:22:14,343
- The hell it is.
- Fake as a six-dollar bill.

356
00:22:14,717 --> 00:22:17,845
- That's not funny, son.
- Take a look back here by the trees...

357
00:22:18,020 --> 00:22:19,510
...the guys on horseback.

358
00:22:20,623 --> 00:22:23,114
Third one in from the left,
next to the footman.

359
00:22:23,292 --> 00:22:24,816
Yeah, yeah, I see the horses.

360
00:22:24,994 --> 00:22:27,861
What do you think,
is that an M-16 or Kalashnikov?

361
00:22:28,330 --> 00:22:31,299
- Son of a bitch.
- Yeah, I think it's a Kalashnikov.

362
00:22:31,467 --> 00:22:33,196
Son of a bitch. There's...

363
00:22:33,369 --> 00:22:36,338
There's a machine gun.
How the hell did you not notice that?

364
00:22:36,639 --> 00:22:39,301
If this bozo here can see it,
why couldn't you?

365
00:22:39,475 --> 00:22:41,943
- A.P., I assure you, the painting...
- No, no, no.

366
00:22:42,111 --> 00:22:44,944
You're the one who has the good eye.
You brought me a fake.

367
00:22:45,114 --> 00:22:47,708
Mr. Caid, I can state
with absolute certainty...

368
00:22:47,883 --> 00:22:52,013
...the painting which I purchased for you
and placed in your hands was genuine.

369
00:22:52,187 --> 00:22:54,621
I have the documents to prove it.

370
00:22:54,790 --> 00:22:58,954
If you chose to engage in some clever
subterfuge without my knowledge...

371
00:22:59,128 --> 00:23:02,359
...I can hardly be blamed
for the results.

372
00:23:03,666 --> 00:23:05,293
- Excuse me.
- Harry.

373
00:23:05,467 --> 00:23:10,063
That son of a bitch Harry. He switched
out the real painting for the fake one.

374
00:23:10,272 --> 00:23:13,241
Then bludgeoned himself to death?
You have no basis for that.

375
00:23:13,409 --> 00:23:15,843
Probably one of his crackhead
musician friends.

376
00:23:16,011 --> 00:23:17,911
You don't know that. That's not fair.

377
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:20,571
Were you aware
he spent time in prison?

378
00:23:20,783 --> 00:23:23,274
Six months for theft in Canada
10 years ago.

379
00:23:25,487 --> 00:23:26,977
Yes.

380
00:23:27,690 --> 00:23:31,490
I mean, no, I didn't.
I'm sure it doesn't...

381
00:23:32,294 --> 00:23:35,058
Yeah, I'm sure it's not...
It was 10 years ago.

382
00:23:35,230 --> 00:23:39,826
I knew it.
I knew this guy was no good for you.

383
00:23:40,002 --> 00:23:42,994
I could feel it.
But I kept quiet, didn't I?

384
00:23:43,172 --> 00:23:46,903
For your sake, I forced myself
to trust this guy...

385
00:23:47,076 --> 00:23:49,169
...because you finally
had yourself a man.

386
00:23:49,611 --> 00:23:52,512
He stole $50,000,000 from me.

387
00:23:53,082 --> 00:23:54,379
Damn it, Stevie.

388
00:23:56,452 --> 00:24:00,218
Please, I beg you,
find my husband's murderer.

389
00:24:01,090 --> 00:24:02,887
That's what we're trying to do,
ma'am.

390
00:24:03,425 --> 00:24:06,758
They'll tell you the truth.
I want the truth.

391
00:24:12,868 --> 00:24:14,199
You can go now.

392
00:24:15,771 --> 00:24:19,434
That's all we need at the moment.
We'll be in touch.

393
00:24:19,608 --> 00:24:21,269
Right.

394
00:24:22,878 --> 00:24:26,109
I hate to be blunt, Mr. Caid,
but you're just...

395
00:24:26,281 --> 00:24:29,944
- Let's... Oh, no, you don't.
- We'll talk later.

396
00:24:30,285 --> 00:24:32,446
Harry has copies made,
but doesn't hang one...

397
00:24:32,621 --> 00:24:35,146
- ...to disguise the robbery. Why?
- Part of the plan.

398
00:24:35,357 --> 00:24:38,155
Everyone has to know
the painting's stolen for him to sell.

399
00:24:38,360 --> 00:24:40,988
- The painting has to be missing.
- He makes the switch...

400
00:24:41,163 --> 00:24:43,222
...steals the real painting
off the wall...

401
00:24:43,398 --> 00:24:45,798
...knowing Frank
has turned the cameras off.

402
00:24:46,368 --> 00:24:47,460
So who kills him, then?

403
00:24:47,636 --> 00:24:49,934
Whoever delivered
the painting to a buyer.

404
00:24:50,105 --> 00:24:54,565
Harry outlived his usefulness
the minute he used his security pass.

405
00:24:54,943 --> 00:24:58,606
Well, we just heard from the
organized crime boys about Shirali Arlov.

406
00:24:58,781 --> 00:25:00,442
- We did?
- Yeah. The good news is...

407
00:25:00,616 --> 00:25:03,244
...he's in California. L.A.
Arrived a couple days ago.

408
00:25:03,418 --> 00:25:05,750
- That can't be a coincidence.
- The bad news?

409
00:25:05,921 --> 00:25:09,288
The bad news is he's traveling
on a diplomatic passport.

410
00:25:09,458 --> 00:25:11,926
We cannot touch him
under any circumstances.

411
00:25:12,094 --> 00:25:15,791
- Just ask him a couple questions.
- State Department was clear. Keep away.

412
00:25:16,198 --> 00:25:18,928
Arlov controls an oil pipeline route
this country needs.

413
00:25:19,101 --> 00:25:20,591
It's a national-security issue.

414
00:25:20,769 --> 00:25:23,101
Another argument
for energy independence there.

415
00:25:23,272 --> 00:25:26,264
- Where's he staying?
- I repeat, do not touch.

416
00:25:27,276 --> 00:25:29,938
Oh, no. No touching.

417
00:25:30,112 --> 00:25:31,841
I promise.

418
00:26:06,548 --> 00:26:10,040
Nope, <i>nyet</i>, no.
Minelli said no too. That's four noes.

419
00:26:10,219 --> 00:26:13,552
- Minelli always says no. It's his job.
- It's my job to listen to him.

420
00:26:13,722 --> 00:26:18,819
You'd walk away? If Arlov is our man,
you'd let him get away with murder?

421
00:26:18,994 --> 00:26:22,327
He didn't murder. He probably
bought the painting from the murderer.

422
00:26:22,497 --> 00:26:23,862
We'll never know.

423
00:26:24,032 --> 00:26:26,728
We're not causing
an international scandal.

424
00:26:26,902 --> 00:26:29,666
- Arlov's the key.
- I wanna get Harry Lashley's killer...

425
00:26:29,838 --> 00:26:33,103
...as much as you do,
but we cannot go after Arlov.

426
00:26:33,275 --> 00:26:35,072
- The State...
- The State Department.

427
00:26:35,244 --> 00:26:36,836
I agree. Screw them.

428
00:26:37,012 --> 00:26:39,674
That attitude is why I'm in charge
and you guys aren't.

429
00:26:39,848 --> 00:26:42,544
Would you please leave
some of those for everybody else?

430
00:26:42,985 --> 00:26:44,850
They're so small.

431
00:26:48,857 --> 00:26:54,090
I just don't understand why a law officer
can't go after a known criminal.

432
00:26:54,763 --> 00:26:56,526
Man, damn right.

433
00:26:58,901 --> 00:27:01,199
If we left now,
we could be in L.A. before dark.

434
00:27:01,370 --> 00:27:02,632
What?

435
00:27:02,804 --> 00:27:05,364
Tell Cho to meet us
in the parking lot in 10 minutes.

436
00:27:25,794 --> 00:27:27,421
- Hello?
- You ready?

437
00:27:28,163 --> 00:27:29,994
- Yeah.
- Are you ready?

438
00:27:30,165 --> 00:27:32,258
I'm ready. What's the signal?

439
00:27:32,668 --> 00:27:35,262
- "She is beautiful."
- Good.

440
00:27:35,671 --> 00:27:36,797
Let's do this.

441
00:28:03,565 --> 00:28:05,430
<i>I'd like to speak to Mr. Arlov, please.</i>

442
00:28:10,072 --> 00:28:11,130
Hi.

443
00:28:11,306 --> 00:28:15,072
Could you tell Mr. Arlov
Patrick Jane is here?

444
00:28:15,711 --> 00:28:18,805
I have the Moro portrait
that was stolen a couple of days ago.

445
00:28:18,981 --> 00:28:21,108
I'd like to sell it to him.

446
00:28:52,314 --> 00:28:53,872
Thank you.

447
00:28:57,886 --> 00:28:59,183
Good evening.

448
00:29:02,057 --> 00:29:03,649
Please sit.

449
00:29:12,934 --> 00:29:15,027
<i>World's Best Collisions.</i>
You know this show?

450
00:29:16,171 --> 00:29:18,571
- No.
- You should check it out. It's...

451
00:29:18,740 --> 00:29:22,198
It's very amusing and educational.

452
00:29:22,377 --> 00:29:27,508
It's a lesson for us all.
Anytime, anyplace, boom:

453
00:29:27,682 --> 00:29:30,173
- Your life can be over.
- That's very true.

454
00:29:30,352 --> 00:29:31,478
Mm-hm.

455
00:29:32,254 --> 00:29:36,452
- Would you like a drink? A snack?
- Uh, yes.

456
00:29:36,658 --> 00:29:38,023
Sparkling water, please.

457
00:29:40,529 --> 00:29:42,156
No ice. Room temperature.

458
00:29:43,365 --> 00:29:45,925
Oh, and in a tall glass, please.

459
00:29:54,376 --> 00:29:57,436
- Where is everyone?
- Weird, they all called in to sign out.

460
00:29:57,612 --> 00:30:01,548
Jane wasn't feeling well, Rigsby's got
a hot date, and Cho got Kings tickets.

461
00:30:01,716 --> 00:30:03,115
- Son of a...
- What?

462
00:30:03,285 --> 00:30:05,947
Jane doesn't get sick,
Rigsby's in love with you...

463
00:30:06,121 --> 00:30:08,453
- ...and the Kings aren't playing.
- You know that?

464
00:30:08,623 --> 00:30:11,285
They're on an East Coast trip,
playing the Knicks.

465
00:30:11,460 --> 00:30:13,291
No, Rigsby.
How do you know about that?

466
00:30:13,462 --> 00:30:16,056
Everybody knows that.
The attorney general knows that.

467
00:30:16,231 --> 00:30:18,290
- I'm so embarrassed.
- Please.

468
00:30:21,269 --> 00:30:23,328
Pick up the phone, damn it.

469
00:30:26,975 --> 00:30:28,499
Thank you.

470
00:30:35,217 --> 00:30:36,980
So...

471
00:30:39,654 --> 00:30:41,781
Mr. Patrick Jane.

472
00:30:43,758 --> 00:30:45,623
You have a painting
you want to sell me?

473
00:30:46,761 --> 00:30:48,023
The Carlotta Moro?

474
00:30:48,797 --> 00:30:51,925
- Yes.
- I don't believe you.

475
00:30:52,167 --> 00:30:55,500
- Would you like to take a look?
- Sure.

476
00:31:13,788 --> 00:31:16,484
- Are you mad?
- No, just kidding.

477
00:31:16,658 --> 00:31:20,150
Kidding.
It's just a prop to get me into the room.

478
00:31:20,328 --> 00:31:22,956
- I'm aware you have the real painting.
- Really?

479
00:31:23,131 --> 00:31:25,122
You are aware.

480
00:31:25,300 --> 00:31:28,030
And how are you aware of this?

481
00:31:28,203 --> 00:31:29,329
A guess.

482
00:31:29,504 --> 00:31:31,665
Confirmed by a predatory smile
on your face...

483
00:31:31,840 --> 00:31:34,138
...when you asked me
about it just then.

484
00:31:34,309 --> 00:31:38,177
You enjoy trapping people
in nets of their own making, don't you?

485
00:31:38,346 --> 00:31:42,373
- That's... That's very perceptive of you.
- Not really.

486
00:31:45,187 --> 00:31:48,315
Most successful criminal bosses
share a similar profile.

487
00:31:48,490 --> 00:31:50,822
Sadistic and violent...

488
00:31:50,992 --> 00:31:55,725
...but also highly methodical
and psychologically astute.

489
00:31:58,366 --> 00:32:00,300
Why are you here, Mr. Jane?

490
00:32:00,835 --> 00:32:03,497
I'm a detective of sorts.

491
00:32:15,350 --> 00:32:17,011
- Explain.
- I'm trying to find out...

492
00:32:17,185 --> 00:32:19,551
- ...who killed Harry Lashley.
- Who?

493
00:32:19,721 --> 00:32:22,087
The man who died
in the course of the theft.

494
00:32:22,257 --> 00:32:24,555
Oh, yes, yes. I recall. Poor fellow.

495
00:32:24,726 --> 00:32:28,526
If you're looking for a bribe,
I'm afraid you're running out of luck.

496
00:32:28,697 --> 00:32:30,028
You see, I...

497
00:32:30,198 --> 00:32:33,634
You do understand that I have immunity
from prosecution in this country.

498
00:32:33,835 --> 00:32:35,359
I know, I know. No touching.

499
00:32:35,537 --> 00:32:38,233
I show you my identification
to discourage you...

500
00:32:38,406 --> 00:32:41,398
...from killing me or torturing me
or something.

501
00:32:41,576 --> 00:32:44,704
You might not get arrested for it,
but you sure will make a fuss.

502
00:32:44,879 --> 00:32:47,040
Oh, I don't mind fuss, you know.

503
00:32:47,215 --> 00:32:49,649
- Anything to avoid boredom.
- Given your immunity...

504
00:32:49,851 --> 00:32:52,786
...you could tell me
who you bought the painting from.

505
00:32:52,954 --> 00:32:55,980
Inform to the police?
Why on earth would I do that?

506
00:32:56,157 --> 00:32:58,557
Harry Lashley didn't deserve to die.

507
00:32:58,727 --> 00:33:01,059
He has a wife
who needs to know what happened.

508
00:33:01,229 --> 00:33:03,163
She deserves justice.

509
00:33:04,299 --> 00:33:07,234
Are you serious? She deserves justice.

510
00:33:07,769 --> 00:33:10,636
Perhaps you haven't paid
the full price of the painting yet.

511
00:33:10,872 --> 00:33:14,638
Perhaps an arrest
would save you some money.

512
00:33:14,809 --> 00:33:16,936
Well, that's not a bad thought.

513
00:33:17,112 --> 00:33:22,607
But you see, I love my art collection.
I love beauty.

514
00:33:22,784 --> 00:33:24,809
It's why I do what I do, you know.

515
00:33:24,986 --> 00:33:28,513
Who will want to sell to me
if I start sending my suppliers to jail?

516
00:33:28,690 --> 00:33:30,157
True.

517
00:33:30,325 --> 00:33:33,317
Well, I thought it was worth a try.

518
00:33:34,663 --> 00:33:36,688
Thank you.
I'm sorry for wasting your time.

519
00:33:36,898 --> 00:33:39,526
Oh, no, no, no. Not at all, not at all.

520
00:33:39,701 --> 00:33:43,762
You are a very strange kind
of policeman.

521
00:33:43,938 --> 00:33:47,271
- It's been most diverting.
- Could I see it before I leave?

522
00:33:48,043 --> 00:33:51,012
<i>The Moro</i>. I'm told it's very beautiful.

523
00:33:51,179 --> 00:33:52,771
Oh, you appreciate art, huh?

524
00:33:54,716 --> 00:33:58,208
Well, why not? Of course.

525
00:34:05,694 --> 00:34:09,027
- What's happening?
- Soon. Get ready.

526
00:34:10,932 --> 00:34:15,665
Here you go. Look at that.
It's an Italian silk merchant's wife.

527
00:34:15,837 --> 00:34:20,638
Six hundred years old, it's...
And it's alive. It's immortal.

528
00:34:21,976 --> 00:34:23,671
May I?

529
00:34:26,047 --> 00:34:27,071
- Please.
- Thanks.

530
00:34:30,051 --> 00:34:33,885
Now I understand why you
went to such lengths to get her.

531
00:34:34,222 --> 00:34:35,951
She is beautiful.

532
00:34:36,157 --> 00:34:37,681
Now.

533
00:34:39,828 --> 00:34:41,728
<i>This is a fire alarm.</i>

534
00:34:41,963 --> 00:34:45,057
<i>Will all hotel guests move toward
the nearest fire exit.</i>

535
00:34:45,233 --> 00:34:46,495
Everybody out.

536
00:34:46,668 --> 00:34:48,363
<i>This is a fire alarm.</i>

537
00:34:48,536 --> 00:34:52,529
Out. Out. Out. Please, out.

538
00:34:52,707 --> 00:34:55,232
Out. Out. Out.

539
00:34:55,477 --> 00:34:57,035
<i>Will all hotel guests</i>...

540
00:34:57,212 --> 00:34:58,406
Out.

541
00:35:07,021 --> 00:35:08,511
- Well?
- Well?

542
00:35:08,690 --> 00:35:10,487
Yes.

543
00:35:11,593 --> 00:35:14,585
- Better call Lisbon. Give her an update.
- You call her.

544
00:35:14,763 --> 00:35:16,526
- I can't, I'm driving. Cho.
- No way.

545
00:35:16,698 --> 00:35:20,566
Oh, come on, guys. I just robbed
a Russian mobster, you can't call Lisbon?

546
00:35:24,806 --> 00:35:27,240
- Are you gonna be grumpy in the room?
- Yes, I am.

547
00:35:27,409 --> 00:35:29,775
Just asking.
I don't think it's very professional.

548
00:35:29,944 --> 00:35:32,435
- Seriously, don't push me.
- I said I'm sorry.

549
00:35:32,614 --> 00:35:35,378
You had express orders
to stay away from Arlov.

550
00:35:35,550 --> 00:35:38,451
- You have total deniability.
- That's beside the point.

551
00:35:38,620 --> 00:35:41,111
The point is my brilliant plan
worked like clockwork.

552
00:35:41,289 --> 00:35:44,417
What plan? This case isn't closed.
You haven't done anything yet.

553
00:35:44,592 --> 00:35:46,457
Not yet, but you watch.

554
00:35:46,661 --> 00:35:48,561
No, I disagree. Mm-hm.

555
00:35:49,664 --> 00:35:50,756
Morning.

556
00:35:51,966 --> 00:35:53,831
I'll get back to you.

557
00:35:54,402 --> 00:35:55,596
Ta-da.

558
00:35:56,237 --> 00:35:58,102
What the hell?

559
00:35:58,273 --> 00:35:59,672
You got it back.

560
00:36:00,442 --> 00:36:02,910
Bring her to me. Oh, baby.

561
00:36:05,246 --> 00:36:07,942
This is fabulous.
Was it Arlov? Did he have it?

562
00:36:08,116 --> 00:36:10,277
- Yes, it was.
- How did you get it back?

563
00:36:10,618 --> 00:36:12,108
Oh, um...

564
00:36:12,487 --> 00:36:14,978
- I stole it.
- Are you serious?

565
00:36:15,156 --> 00:36:17,624
There was nothing we could do legally,
as you said.

566
00:36:17,792 --> 00:36:20,090
Arlov was in possession
of a stolen painting...

567
00:36:20,261 --> 00:36:22,525
...so there was nothing
he could do either.

568
00:36:22,697 --> 00:36:24,494
This gets better and better.

569
00:36:24,666 --> 00:36:26,691
First of all, I get the painting back.

570
00:36:26,868 --> 00:36:31,202
Second, when Arlov finds out
that I've got it, it'll kill him.

571
00:36:31,372 --> 00:36:32,930
Who cares about Arlov?

572
00:36:33,107 --> 00:36:35,940
Who cares about the damn painting?

573
00:36:37,178 --> 00:36:39,373
You assured me
you would find Harry's killer.

574
00:36:39,581 --> 00:36:42,880
We're doing everything we can,
but there's not a lot left for us.

575
00:36:43,084 --> 00:36:44,881
Look, Stevie, I know you loved him.

576
00:36:45,086 --> 00:36:47,554
I liked him too,
but he was part of this theft.

577
00:36:47,722 --> 00:36:48,984
Now, he betrayed me.

578
00:36:49,157 --> 00:36:51,387
In a way, this is a good thing
that he's gone.

579
00:36:51,593 --> 00:36:54,494
Because you could be having children
with this bum right now.

580
00:36:54,662 --> 00:36:56,994
All right? You think of that.
We dodged a bullet.

581
00:36:57,165 --> 00:36:59,292
Come on. Give me a hug.

582
00:37:00,134 --> 00:37:02,364
Look, we're gonna find you
a good man.

583
00:37:02,737 --> 00:37:05,900
The main thing is
we sent out a message here...

584
00:37:06,207 --> 00:37:09,404
...that nobody messes with A.P. Caid.

585
00:37:09,611 --> 00:37:11,408
Thank you. Thank you.

586
00:37:21,356 --> 00:37:22,914
I had a daughter once.

587
00:37:26,060 --> 00:37:27,550
She died.

588
00:37:27,729 --> 00:37:29,924
Killed. It was my fault.

589
00:37:31,199 --> 00:37:34,396
I know that a man who treats his
daughter the way you do is a fool.

590
00:37:34,569 --> 00:37:36,059
- Jane.
- What'd you say?

591
00:37:36,237 --> 00:37:39,172
Your child is hurting and you're
crowing over this victory...

592
00:37:39,340 --> 00:37:41,035
...like a cockerel on a dung heap.

593
00:37:41,943 --> 00:37:45,606
You're a blind, vain,
emotionally stunted fool.

594
00:37:45,780 --> 00:37:47,748
You care more about this...

595
00:37:47,916 --> 00:37:52,546
- For this painting, for all this rubbish.
- Than you do the child that loves you.

596
00:37:53,922 --> 00:37:56,254
You found my painting, I am grateful.

597
00:37:56,424 --> 00:38:01,794
And now I'm going to be patient and
tell you get this clown out of my office.

598
00:38:01,963 --> 00:38:04,625
How am I gonna make you understand?
How am I?

599
00:38:04,799 --> 00:38:06,562
- See this? See this?
- Jane, let's...

600
00:38:06,734 --> 00:38:08,702
This is nothing. It's nothing.

601
00:38:09,971 --> 00:38:11,461
- Hey.
- No. No.

602
00:38:11,673 --> 00:38:13,402
- What, are you nuts?
- Oh, my God.

603
00:38:13,575 --> 00:38:15,270
Jane. Jane, have you gone crazy?

604
00:38:15,443 --> 00:38:16,910
No, no, no. Just doing my job.

605
00:38:17,078 --> 00:38:19,876
Look. Someone that's dedicated
their life to art...

606
00:38:20,048 --> 00:38:22,608
...would wanna save a masterpiece
from burning up.

607
00:38:22,784 --> 00:38:25,150
Unless you already knew
it was just another fake.

608
00:38:25,320 --> 00:38:28,289
No, not at all, I'm just...
I'm just shocked, that's all.

609
00:38:28,723 --> 00:38:30,657
- Is it a fake?
- Yes, it is.

610
00:38:30,825 --> 00:38:33,453
I only found out after I stole it,
but Kathryn knew...

611
00:38:33,628 --> 00:38:35,892
...because it was she
who stole the original.

612
00:38:36,064 --> 00:38:40,433
And it is she who still has it
in her safe possession.

613
00:38:41,402 --> 00:38:42,960
- No.
- That true, Kathryn?

614
00:38:43,137 --> 00:38:46,971
- No.
- Horrible job you found for yourself.

615
00:38:47,775 --> 00:38:51,108
Helping a greedy egotist
snatch up beautiful things...

616
00:38:51,279 --> 00:38:53,270
...he has no ability to appreciate.

617
00:38:53,748 --> 00:38:56,342
And you don't get paid much, do you?

618
00:38:56,517 --> 00:39:01,079
All the money, beauty around you,
and you can't have any of it.

619
00:39:02,090 --> 00:39:03,216
Very frustrating.

620
00:39:03,958 --> 00:39:05,220
Not at all.

621
00:39:06,461 --> 00:39:08,986
Harry came to you for advice
about the painting.

622
00:39:09,364 --> 00:39:13,858
You asked him to make a second copy
for your own personal use.

623
00:39:14,469 --> 00:39:16,903
You blackmail him? Huh?

624
00:39:17,772 --> 00:39:20,263
Must've known
about the theft conviction in Canada.

625
00:39:20,742 --> 00:39:24,974
Threatened to tell A.P. and destroy
the life he was building with Stevie.

626
00:39:25,179 --> 00:39:26,510
Care to comment on that?

627
00:39:26,748 --> 00:39:29,683
- It's all nonsense, A.P. It's all nonsense.
- Oh, is it?

628
00:39:29,851 --> 00:39:33,514
The reason you had two copies made
so that you could sell one to Arlov...

629
00:39:33,688 --> 00:39:37,180
...and you could keep
the original picture for yourself.

630
00:39:37,659 --> 00:39:41,618
Because, of course, you're the only one
that really deserves it.

631
00:39:41,796 --> 00:39:43,696
It was her? She did it?

632
00:39:43,865 --> 00:39:46,299
Yes. Like I switched the paintings
with Arlov...

633
00:39:46,467 --> 00:39:51,632
...she switched the original with Harry
right before it went into the vault.

634
00:39:51,806 --> 00:39:55,242
All you had to do
was come up in here after hours...

635
00:39:55,410 --> 00:39:57,844
...and take it off the wall.

636
00:40:01,883 --> 00:40:03,851
<i>But Harry suspected something,
didn't he?</i>

637
00:40:04,018 --> 00:40:06,077
<i>He tried to stop you.</i>

638
00:40:16,531 --> 00:40:18,658
<i>And you killed him.</i>

639
00:40:19,200 --> 00:40:22,601
Tell the truth. Our people are going
through your apartment right now.

640
00:40:22,804 --> 00:40:27,036
They will find that painting even if they
have to tear the place to the ground.

641
00:40:27,208 --> 00:40:29,676
There is no point in lying anymore.

642
00:40:31,512 --> 00:40:33,173
I'm sorry.

643
00:40:34,115 --> 00:40:35,776
I never meant to kill him.

644
00:40:35,950 --> 00:40:38,578
- Why did he try to stop me?
- No.

645
00:40:38,753 --> 00:40:40,744
- No, no. No.
- It was an accident.

646
00:40:40,922 --> 00:40:43,447
You're under arrest
for the murder of Harry Lashley.

647
00:40:43,624 --> 00:40:46,184
- Put your hands behind your back.
- I'm so sorry.

648
00:40:46,360 --> 00:40:48,419
Come on, let's go.

649
00:40:55,203 --> 00:40:58,263
For what it's worth, I...

650
00:40:58,573 --> 00:41:01,770
I just wanted to apologize
for my pious rant earlier.

651
00:41:01,943 --> 00:41:08,576
I needed a pretext to burn the picture,
and your family dysfunction became it.

652
00:41:08,750 --> 00:41:12,049
Well, no, sir. No apology needed.

653
00:41:12,220 --> 00:41:14,245
There was some hard truth
in what you said.

654
00:41:14,422 --> 00:41:18,358
Yes, there was. Well, you know,
no one likes to be too judgmental.

655
00:41:28,669 --> 00:41:31,570
- Hard to believe it's a fake.
- Crazy, isn't it?

656
00:41:32,807 --> 00:41:36,504
One painting's worth 50 million,
the other's only worth a few thousand.

657
00:41:37,378 --> 00:41:38,470
They look identical.

658
00:41:38,913 --> 00:41:40,676
Yup. Crazy.

659
00:41:42,884 --> 00:41:45,910
Now that you mention it,
I'm not sure...

660
00:41:51,626 --> 00:41:54,094
It's just so hard to keep track
after a while.

